---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { OPERATOR } from '../../lib/config';
// Public incident and App Review record (Rick, 2026-11-27: "pull all info into our public disclosures, along with recent apple rejections").
// Rules: dated facts we can show; name no person, venue, network address or other company; no personal data.
// Apple's messages are summarised by guideline number, never quoted.
---
<Base title="Incidents and App Review: the record" description="A dated public record of service incidents on Hot Mess and AudienceKit, what we changed, and Apple's recent App Review decisions.">
  <main>
    <section class="wrap record-article">
      <p class="eyebrow">On the record · Updated November 27, 2026</p>
      <h1>Incidents and App Review: the record</h1>
      <div class="tldr">
        <p><strong>TL;DR.</strong> Hot Mess is years of work and countless hours and dollars of my own. It provides its services without ads and without selling data, and it is built to make our community better. Attacking it, or listings like free food on Thanksgiving, has real-world impact on access to food, shelter and community, and a lack of connection can be part of why some people end their lives. Because of that, I have a zero-tolerance policy for this behavior, and anyone who does it should expect to be publicly shamed for it. <em>— Rick Mark</em></p>
        <p class="crisis">If you are struggling or thinking about ending your life, you are not alone. In the US, call or text 988 to reach the Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline, any time.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="lede">When something goes wrong with the service, we write down what happened, when, and what we changed. All times are Mountain Time.</p>

      <h2>A statement from Rick Mark</h2>
      <p>Hot Mess is free. This week that included a free directory of holiday meals for anyone in our community who needed one. Flooding a free community service with hundreds of thousands of requests isn’t a prank or a test. It puts at risk the people who depend on that service, on the week many of them need it most.</p>
      <p>The bug behind this traffic was known, and the fix was on the App Store by the evening of November 25. The same sign-in kept flooding us from the same network for another full day after that. A bug explains a loop. In my view, it doesn’t explain choosing to keep one running after the fix is out. I believe this flood was deliberate, and that it is part of a campaign against my work that has gone on for a long time. Whoever chose to do this: it didn’t work. The service stayed up, the limits held, and every change listed on this page makes the next attempt harder. Attacks like this don’t make our community smaller. They make it easier to see who is working to keep people fed and safe, and who is working against them.</p>
      <p>If you know anything about this traffic, write to me. If you need a holiday meal, the directory is open.</p>
      <p><strong>Why I believe it, as a security researcher.</strong> I have worked on Apple platforms since 2007 and on hardware security research for most of that time. With Team t8012 I helped bring Apple’s T2 security chip into checkra1n, and I have worked at Meta, Coinbase, Dropbox, Uber ATG, Bloomberg and Microsoft. In my professional judgment this traffic was deliberate. Its volume and timing don’t fit any client bug I have seen, and it fits a pattern of my work being copied and attacked over many years. <em>— Rick Mark, Love Wins Media</em></p>

      <h2>November 24 to 26, 2026: our service flooded through a known flaw</h2>
      <p>An older release of Hot Mess for iPhone, version 2.8, has a flaw that makes a screen reload itself over and over, and that flaw could be used to flood our service. From November 23 to 26, one outside network flooded us through it. At its peak it sent about 143,000 requests an hour. Over one 24-hour stretch it sent about 896,000 requests, 96% of all the traffic our API received.</p>
      <p><strong>The flooding went on for a full day after the fix was on the App Store.</strong> Apart from this one sign-in, we saw no App Store install trigger the flaw in everyday use.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Nov 23, about 11:20 PM to Nov 24, 12:05 AM:</strong> the first wave slowed the API for everyone, and some people saw errors in the app.</li>
        <li><strong>Nov 24, 7:23 PM:</strong> our logging named the source: one member’s sign-in on Hot Mess for iPhone 2.8.</li>
        <li><strong>By the evening of Nov 25:</strong> Hot Mess 2.9, which closes the flaw, was on the App Store.</li>
        <li><strong>Nov 26, about 2 AM to 8 AM and 7 PM to 10 PM:</strong> two more waves from the same network, after the fix was available. Our limits held, and the service stayed up.</li>
        <li><strong>Nov 26, 11 PM:</strong> the flooding stopped. None has come from that network since.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Whatever its cause, traffic at this volume is abuse of the service. We will block any network that repeats it, and we keep the records needed to report it.</p>
      <p><strong>How sure we are.</strong> We publish what our records make probable and label the rest.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Probable:</strong> every request used one member’s single sign-in, through the 2.8 flaw, from one network. That sign-in was made over a mobile network, on one phone that no other account has used.</li>
        <li><strong>Probable:</strong> the volume was far beyond anything the flaw produced anywhere else.</li>
        <li><strong>Our belief, not proven:</strong> that the flooding was deliberate. We believe it was because it kept going after the fix shipped. An install that was never updated would also keep looping, so our records can’t settle it.</li>
        <li><strong>Possible, not shown:</strong> that the sign-in was copied to another machine. A copied sign-in would look the same in our records, because we don’t log a device on every request.</li>
        <li><strong>Unknown:</strong> who was holding the device.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>What was exposed.</strong> The requests we could identify were the app’s ordinary screen-layout request, which returns the venue and event cards the app shows. We have found no sign of access beyond what the app itself shows a member.</p>
      <p><strong>What we changed.</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Nov 24, 12:07 AM: per-caller request limits switched from warning to enforcing. A caller over its limit gets a quick “slow down” answer instead of tying up the servers.</li>
        <li>Nov 24, 1:13 AM: over-limit callers are refused before any sign-in or database work, and every refusal is logged with the app and version that made it.</li>
        <li>Every refused request is now kept in a log for 30 days.</li>
        <li>Our websites get their own request budget, so a flood from elsewhere can’t lock them out.</li>
        <li>Our app toolkit now paces its own requests and backs off when the server says to slow down (AudienceKit SDK 0.110.0).</li>
        <li>App Review’s demo accounts are never limited, so a flood can’t interfere with Apple’s review.</li>
        <li>Hot Mess 2.9 and 2.10 for iPhone, which close the flaw, are on the App Store.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>How we found the flaw.</strong> Our own test builds looped the same way on our own phones on Nov 18 and again on Nov 24, during development. That is how we found the flaw and built the fix, and we list it here because those builds were ours.</p>

      <h2>This has happened before: June 2017</h2>
      <p>In June 2017, Facebook rate-limited Hot Mess’s Facebook app, which disrupted signing in with Facebook. Our records from that month show the response:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>June 10, 2017:</strong> we cut how often Hot Mess refreshed public Facebook pages from every 12 hours to every 36, stopped refreshing hidden pages, and rotated our Facebook app secret.</li>
        <li><strong>June 13, 2017:</strong> we moved to a newer, fixed version of Facebook’s API.</li>
        <li><strong>June 19, 2017:</strong> pages whose last refresh failed are retried sooner, and all others wait their turn.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Both times, a burst of requests that ordinary use doesn’t explain ran into limits meant to protect the service. Both times, we answered by pacing our own requests and tightening our limits.</p>

      <h2>November 27, 2026: holiday pages failing on this site</h2>
      <p>Just after midnight, eight page loads on hotmess.social failed with “cities unavailable” or “directory unavailable” between 12:01 and 12:06 AM. Each failed in about 2 milliseconds, without reaching our API, while other loads in the same minutes worked.</p>
      <p><strong>Cause.</strong> Our own bug. One copy of the site’s server kept a failed lookup in memory and handed it to later visitors instead of asking again. The one failure we examined in full was served from Cloudflare’s Denver location. That is the data center nearest the visitor, not where any traffic came from.</p>
      <p><strong>What we changed.</strong> Each page load now gets its own connection, nothing is shared between visitors, and holiday pages are cached, with a week-old good copy served if the API is down.</p>

      <h2>Recent App Review decisions</h2>
      <p>These are Apple’s decisions on our apps since October, from App Store Connect. Guideline numbers refer to Apple’s App Review Guidelines.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Velvet for iPhone and iPad, Oct 21:</strong> 2.1, App Review couldn’t sign in with our review code. We fixed the code.</li>
        <li><strong>Velvet, Oct 23:</strong> 2.1(b), questions about our business model. Velvet sells nothing, and we answered the same day.</li>
        <li><strong>Velvet, Nov 5:</strong> 2.1 and 2.5.4, questions about comment moderation and background audio for AirPlay. We answered with a demo video.</li>
        <li><strong>Velvet, Nov 13:</strong> 4.3 (spam) and 4.2.6, 12 seconds after review began. We answered every question and appealed to the App Review Board. <a href="/record/velvet">The full Velvet record</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Velvet 1.10, Nov 23:</strong> 2.5.4 (background audio) and 2.1(a) (access). We removed background audio and resubmitted the same day. It has been waiting for review since Nov 22, 8:58 PM.</li>
        <li><strong>Velvet for Apple TV 1.0, Nov 25:</strong> 3.1.1 (the TV asked for a code to be typed in) and 2.1(a) (pairing). The TV now only shows a code, and you approve it in a browser. We resubmitted the same day.</li>
        <li><strong>Velvet for Mac 1.0:</strong> waiting for review since Nov 19.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>In the same period Apple approved Hot Mess 2.2 through 2.10, Focus, Backstage, Soapbox and Arbiter.</p>

      <p class="muted">Questions about this record: <a href={`mailto:${OPERATOR.email}`}>{OPERATOR.email}</a>. Our dated record of Hot Mess’s development: <a href="/record/hot-mess-dated-record.pdf">hot-mess-dated-record.pdf</a>.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

